  "query_preview": "..\n\nSignificant tropospheric moistening was sampled via the 01.00Z KDTX \nRAOB, marked by vertical continuity in saturation and a measured \nPWAT of 1.70 inches. This modified tropically sourced air mass has \nallowed overnight showers to percolate with ease beneath sustained \nperturbed flow aloft. The latest forcing mechanism has been \nattributed to a series of shortwave impulses positioned ahead of an \ninbound longwave trough moving eastward over southern Canada, and \ndownstream of a southern CONUS closed low. The southern wave has \nbeen responsible for the influx of eastern Pacific and Gulf \nmoisture, upholding the appropriate environment for this extended \nperiod of rainfall.\n\nThe low-level jet moisture connection is fully engaged early this \nmorning, with both increasing magnitude (30+ knots) and trajectory \ncontinuity. Latest progs indicate the confluent stream arcs back to \nTexas where the remnants of Hurricane Polo merge with Gulf-wide \ntheta-e advection. Additionally, the two synoptic waves will begin \nto phase during the PM hours, leading to a rise in upper-level \ndivergence and local theta-e convergence. This lends continued \nconcern for the localized risk of heavy rainfall later today, \nespecially as a cold front extending from the northern system tracks \nsoutheastward across Lower Michigan. The period of greatest concern \nwill be as the frontal slope engages the peak in low-level moisture \ntransport late this afternoon into the evening timeframe,  while the \nsystem's deformation axis centers overhead. See the Hydrology \nsection for additional details.\n\nLatest NWP suite indicates peak 3-hourly rainfall rates between 00Z \nand 06Z tonight as the front pushes through. Winds flip \nnorthwesterly immediately behind the surface front tonight, although \nshowers will be slower to clear. Once aggressive post-frontal system-\nrelative isentropic downglide builds in, rainfall will quickly \nconclude. Dry weather is then forecast by mid-morning Friday as the \nGreat Lakes reside downstream of building 1028 mb high pressure \nmoving into the Upper Midwest. Seasonably cool and dry as the ridge \ndominates. A clipper system could bring a few sprinkles through \nSunday morning, while thermal troughing persists. 850 mb \ntemperatures crater early next week, with sub-0C values possible. \nMorning frost concerns may arise should lows drop into the upper 30s.",
